The '''1994 Georgia gubernatorial election''' occurred on November&nbsp;8, 1994, to elect the next [[List of governors of Georgia|governor of Georgia]] from 1995 to 1999. Incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] governor [[Zell Miller]], first elected in [[Georgia gubernatorial election, 1990|1990]], ran for a second term. In his party's primary, Miller received three challengers, but easily prevailed with just over 70% of the vote. The contest for the Republican nomination, however, was a competitive race. As no candidate received a majority of the vote, John Knox and [[Guy Millner]] advanced to a run-off election. Millner was victorious and received the Republican nomination after garnering 59.41% of the vote.

The general election was a competitive race between Zell Miller and Guy Millner. Issues such as [[welfare reform]], education, and the removal of the [[Flags of the Confederate States of America#Battle flag|Confederate battle flag]] from [[Flag of Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia's state flag]] dominated the election. On election day, Miller defeated Millner 51.05%-48.95% in the third-closest gubernatorial election in Georgian history – behind only the [[2018 Georgia gubernatorial election|2018]] and [[Georgia gubernatorial election, 1966|1966 elections]] – since Reconstruction due to the [[Republican Revolution|strong Republican wave of 1994]]. Although the state was becoming increasingly more Republican, Democrats would retain the governor's mansion [[Georgia gubernatorial election, 2002|until 2003]]. As of 2022, this is the last time [[Catoosa County, Georgia|Catoosa County]] voted for the Democratic candidate for governor.

==Background==
Incumbent governor [[Zell Miller]] (D) chose to run for re-election in 1994. Early in his first term, Miller's approval rating fell significantly after attempting to gain legislative support for removing the [[Flags of the Confederate States of America#Battle flag|Confederate battle flag]] from [[Flag of Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia's state flag]].<ref name="brown">{{cite web|url=http://citation.allacademic.com/meta/p_mla_apa_research_citation/3/0/7/2/5/pages307253/p307253-13.php|title=Policy Context and the Racialization of Welfare Reform|author=Hana E. Brown|publisher=[[University of California, Berkeley]]|page=13|access-date=March 21, 2015|location=Berkeley, California|archive-date=April 2, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402125351/http://citation.allacademic.com/meta/p_mla_apa_research_citation/3/0/7/2/5/pages307253/p307253-13.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> Miller wanted the Confederate battle flag removed before [[Atlanta]] hosted [[Super Bowl XXVIII]] and the [[1996 Summer Olympics]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1992-05-29-mn-308-story.html|title=Georgia Governor Wants to Lower Confederate Flag: South: Under pressure from civil rights groups, he will push for removal of battle cross from state banner|author=Eric Harrison and Edith Stanley|date=May 29, 1992|newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]]|access-date=March 21, 2015|location=Atlanta, Georgia}}</ref> but backed down in March&nbsp;1993 after it became apparent that the [[Georgia House of Representatives]] would not support this proposal.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/03/10/us/south-s-emblem-to-be-retained-on-georgia-flag.html|title=South's Emblem To Be Retained On Georgia Flag|date=March 10, 1993|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=March 21, 2015}}</ref> However, following the same legislative session, Miller's popularity began to recover after bills were passed that initiated his [[welfare reform]] proposals. Miller's disapproval promptly decreased to 29% and fell further to 25% about a year later.<ref name="brown"/>

A week prior to the scheduled primary elections on July&nbsp;12, 14&nbsp;polling locations in the southwestern portion of the state were either underwater or serving as emergency shelters as a result of the disastrous flooding wrought by [[Tropical Storm Alberto (1994)|Tropical Storm Alberto]]. State officials began discussing delaying the primary elections. However, on July&nbsp;19, turnout exceeded predictions.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.albanyherald.com/news/2014/jun/27/the-flood-of-1994-a-day-by-day-account/|title=The Flood of 1994, a day-by-day account|date=June 27, 2014|newspaper=[[The Albany Herald]]|access-date=March 21, 2015}}</ref>

==Democratic primary==

===Campaign===
Despite pledging in 1990 to serve only one term, incumbent governor Zell Miller announced his re-election bid on June&nbsp;16, 1993.<ref name="millerannozunce">{{cite news|url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/1993/06/18/georgia-governor-breaks-vow-not-to-seek-2nd-term/|title=Georgia Governor Breaks Vow Not To Seek 2nd Term|date=June 18, 1993|newspaper=[[Orlando Sentinel]]|access-date=March 21, 2015|location=Atlanta, Georgia}}</ref> During the next 12&nbsp;months, three other Democrats entered the primary, they included [[perennial candidate]] Jim Boyd, State Representative Charles "Judy" Poag, and Korean War veteran Mark Tate. Boyd and Poag attacked Miller for attempting to change the state flag and increasing fees for driver's licenses, car tags, and sporting licenses. Miller defended other portions of his record, including the establishment of the [[Georgia Lottery]], the passage of a $100&nbsp;million tax cut, and a bill proposed that would take a tougher stance on violent criminals. In May&nbsp;1994, Mark Tate was convicted of attempting defrauding the [[United States Department of Veterans Affairs|Department of Veterans Affairs]] out of $99,000 by falsely claiming he had no other source of income. His sentencing was scheduled for July&nbsp;19, the day of the primary. However, Tate's name remained on the ballot.<ref name="ctgcn">{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=YXcwAAAAIBAJ&sjid=SzoDAAAAIBAJ&pg=6914%2C7162347|title=Lance: Who Will Face Zell?|author=John M. Willis|date=June 22, 1994|newspaper=Calhoun Times and Gordon County News|pages=7|access-date=March 21, 2015}}</ref>

===Candidates===
*[[Zell Miller]], incumbent [[governor of Georgia]]
* Jim Boyd, candidate for [[Georgia's 9th congressional district]] in [[United States House of Representatives elections, 1976|1976]] and [[United States House of Representatives elections, 1982|1982]], [[United States Senate election in Georgia, 1984|1984 United States Senate election]], and [[Georgia Public Service Commission]]er in 1986 and 1988
* Mark Tate, [[Korean War]] veteran
* Charles "Judy" Poag, [[Georgia House of Representatives|Georgia state representative]] from [[Eton, Georgia|Eton]]

